.TH CCOL_DYNAMIC_QUEUE_CREATE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynamic_queue_create \- create a dynamic queue with no size limit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_dynamic_queue_create(err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro creates a queue with no size limit. A linked list holds the
queue. A send never blocks, and the queue can grow with no limit. The macro
uses the standard malloc and free.

This macro is the short form of ccol_dynamic_queue_create_with_mprocs()
with NULL memory management procedures.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
err_str  Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the macro gives a pointer to the new dynamic queue.
On failure, it gives NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations on this queue are thread-safe.
You must destroy the queue with ccol_dynamic_queue_destroy() after you
finish with it.
There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with this queue. The first
way is to call fork(2) before you create the queue. The second way is to
create the queue, then call fork(2) and immediately call exec(3). One
pattern is not supported: a program continues to run with no exec(3) in
between while a queue from before the fork is still live. On top of that
rule, the library adds a defense in depth. The child never inherits a lock
in the locked state from another thread that held it at the instant of the
fork. Also, the queue owns no thread of its own. A queue that a child
inherits in that way therefore stays fully usable.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_dynamic_queue_create_with_mprocs (3),
.BR ccol_dynamic_queue_destroy (3)
